Z||000008||Dan||05-14-2000||09:05 AM|||| Been there done that.The rears when you reinsert the caliper must be turnrd as it is inserted because the piston is threadad on the back to the e-brake lever, tahts how the brake works.Try getting the piston out and trying it again.If you have an automatic, I recommend the Baretta Brake upgrade, it is a direct bolt on for the rear, but you loose the e-brake. There are valves to be inserted for e-brake.Did I mention the Baretta parts are cheaper than the Fiero parts, and better.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||05-14-2000||09:30 AM||||Will the rear (stock) calipers work on the front??  I have a brand new set of rear calipers (long story).  The front ones are original (I assume from looking at them).  I plan to do the Beretta/GA upgrade, but I'd hate to waste the new calipers.  (BTW--I want to do the rears 1st since they're cheaper.  No machine work, new studs, etc)||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-14-2000||10:57 AM||||I believe the piston may have come out because you are suposed to install the e brake arm before you remove the blocking.<P>By now odds are good you ruined the caliper.||||reg||1
